<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ptolemy XII Auletes</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 117–<a href="/years/51-bce">51 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Ptolemy XII</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ptolemy XII Auletes was king of Ptolemaic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> from 80 to 58 BCE and again from 55 to 51 BCE, and father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleopatra-vii">Cleopatra VII</a>. Dependent on Roman goodwill, he paid huge sums to Roman politicians for recognition, was driven out by the Alexandrians in 58 BCE, and was restored in 55 BCE by the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aulus-gabinius">Aulus Gabinius</a>, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ptolemy-xii-auletes.jpg" alt="Ptolemy XII Auletes" width="320" height="485" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marie-Lan Nguyen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ptolemy_XII_Auletes_Louvre_Ma3449.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
